{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0195gLtnYt4vhfKW7dqFrtqJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HKj8pyQxsWW7NYdpvA62gb","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_18/projects/schema-dag-extraction/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T053106/best/paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11126,"cache_read_input_tokens":25219,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11126,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XQmFj894dbsHdJf95YxY3H","session_id":"99db7f36-0c41-4b00-81a4-b83fd43f6915","uuid":"042092dd-e04a-4bd6-8391-0f8fea57cd17"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HKj8pyQxsWW7NYdpvA62gb","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n6\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n7\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n8\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n12\t\n13\t\\title{Multi-Channel Schema Degradation in Training-Free LLM Information Extraction:\\\\Isolating Confusability from Count and Operationalizing SDGD}\n14\t\n15\t\\author{Anonymous}\n16\t\n17\t\\begin{document}\n18\t\\maketitle\n19\t\n20\t% ============================================================\n21\t\\begin{abstract}\n22\tTraining-free LLM information extraction degrades severely on complex schemas, yet the mechanisms driving this degradation remain poorly understood.\n23\tWe conduct the first controlled study isolating \\emph{type confusability} from \\emph{type count} in schema-conditioned LLM IE.\n24\tAcross two tasks (NER on Few-NERD with 66 types; Event Detection on MAVEN with 168 types) and two model families (Qwen3-32B, LLaMA-3.3-70B), we establish a \\textbf{multi-channel degradation model}: count drives recall decline through capacity bottleneck (Channel~1), while confusability drives type confusion errors through semantic interference (Channel~2).\n25\tBatch confusability score (BCS) independently predicts F1 degradation after controlling for type frequency (partial $r{=}0.32$, $p{=}0.002$ on Few-NERD; $r{=}0.22$, $p{=}0.004$ on MAVEN), with high-confusability batches exhibiting 10--28$\\times$ more type substitution errors.\n26\tWe operationalize these findings into \\textbf{Schema-DAG Guided Decomposition (SDGD)}, a graph-coloring framework that partitions types into low-confusability batches.\n27\tSDGD improves F1 by 7.6 points on Few-NERD and 6.0 points on MAVEN over single-pass extraction, matching per-type splitting quality at 22\\% of the latency cost.\n28\t\\end{abstract}\n29\t\n30\t% ============================================================\n31\t\\section{Introduction}\n32\t\\label{sec:intro}\n33\t\n34\tLarge language models have become practical tools for training-free information extraction, enabling deployment in domains where annotated data is scarce or nonexistent---clinical records~\\cite{}, regulatory filings, and multilingual settings covering 50+ languages with no IE corpora.\n35\tHowever, performance degrades sharply as schema complexity increases: a 20--60 F1 gap separates training-free LLM IE from supervised systems on schemas with dozens of fine-grained types.\n36\t\n37\tExisting decomposition strategies address this gap with ad-hoc heuristics.\n38\tPer-type splitting~\\cite{} processes each type independently, eliminating inter-type confusion but losing cross-type context and incurring linear API cost.\n39\tSimilarity-based partitioning~\\cite{} uses type embeddings as implicit confusability proxies without empirical validation.\n40\tFixed pipeline decomposition~\\cite{} operates at the task level, ignoring intra-layer schema structure.\n41\tNone of these approaches isolates \\emph{what} makes schemas complex for LLMs.\n42\t\n43\tWe hypothesize that schema-induced degradation is a \\emph{multi-channel} process.\n44\t\\textbf{Channel~1} (Count $\\to$ Recall): more types in the prompt dilute attention, causing entity omission.\n45\t\\textbf{Channel~2} (Confusability $\\to$ Type Confusion): semantically similar co-present types interfere during type assignment, causing correct spans to receive wrong labels.\n46\tThese channels are orthogonal: reducing batch size addresses Channel~1; separating confusable types addresses Channel~2; the gains are additive.\n47\t\n48\tWe validate this model through three layers of evidence:\n49\t\\begin{enumerate}\n50\t    \\item \\textbf{Pairwise BCS-confusion correlation} (immune to type frequency confounds) confirms that embedding-based confusability predicts empirical type confusion ($r{=}0.16$--$0.19$, $p{<}10^{-9}$) across 2 datasets and 2 models.\n51\t    \\item \\textbf{Per-type partial correlation} shows confusability predicts F1 degradation after controlling for type frequency (partial $r{\\geq}0.22$, $p{<}0.01$).\n52\t    \\item \\textbf{Error taxonomy} provides mechanistic evidence: type substitution errors rise monotonically with BCS (8\\%$\\to$68\\%), while false negatives decline (62\\%$\\to$15\\%), cleanly separating the two channels ($\\chi^2{=}142.3$, $p{=}2.1{\\times}10^{-24}$).\n53\t\\end{enumerate}\n54\t\n55\tWe then operationalize these findings into \\textbf{Schema-DAG Guided Decomposition (SDGD)}.\n56\tSDGD constructs a confusability graph where edges connect type pairs above an SCS threshold, then applies graph coloring to partition types into minimally confusable batches.\n57\tSDGD-zero (embedding-only, no calibration) yields +7.6 F1 on Few-NERD and +6.0 on MAVEN over single-pass baselines.\n58\tSDGD-cal (with 8--10 calibration examples) further improves to +9.1 and +8.0.\n59\tBoth variants match or exceed per-type splitting quality at 15--23\\% of the API cost.\n60\t\n61\t% ============================================================\n62\t\\section{Related Work}\n63\t\\label{sec:related}\n64\t\n65\t\\paragraph{Training-Free LLM IE.}\n66\tRecent work has demonstrated that LLMs can perform information extraction in a zero-shot or few-shot manner.\n67\tGoLLIE~\\cite{} uses code-style schemas; UniNER~\\cite{} adopts per-type prompting to handle large type inventories.\n68\tLLM-PEE~\\cite{} partitions types by embedding similarity.\n69\tProUIE~\\cite{} and KGGen~\\cite{} use multi-step pipelines that decompose extraction by task (e.g., entity detection then typing) rather than by schema structure.\n70\t\n71\t\\paragraph{Schema Complexity Effects.}\n72\tThe negative impact of label-set size on extraction quality has been noted in passing~\\cite{}, but no prior work has conducted controlled experiments isolating count from confusability.\n73\tThe closest precedent is the ``Lost in the Middle'' study on position-dependent degradation in long contexts, which demonstrated that controlled experimental design can reveal systematic failure modes in LLMs.\n74\t\n75\t\\paragraph{Type Confusability in IE.}\n76\tType confusion has been studied in supervised IE through confusion matrices and hierarchical label schemes.\n77\tIn training-free settings, confusability has been used implicitly (e.g., embedding-based grouping in LLM-PEE) but never validated empirically as a predictor of extraction degradation independent of type count.\n78\t\n79\t% ============================================================\n80\t\\section{Method}\n81\t\\label{sec:method}\n82\t\n83\t\\subsection{Problem Setting}\n84\tGiven an input document $d$ and a schema $\\mathcal{S} = \\{t_1, \\ldots, t_n\\}$ of $n$ entity/event types, training-free IE prompts an LLM $\\mathcal{M}$ to extract all mentions of types in $\\mathcal{S}$ from $d$.\n85\tWe study how extraction quality varies as a function of $n$ and the semantic similarity structure within $\\mathcal{S}$.\n86\t\n87\t\\subsection{Confusability Measurement}\n88\t\\label{sec:scs}\n89\t\n90\t\\paragraph{Schema Confusability Score (SCS).}\n91\tFor each type pair $(t_i, t_j)$, we compute the cosine similarity between their label embeddings using a sentence encoder:\n92\t\\begin{equation}\n93\t    \\text{SCS}(t_i, t_j) = \\cos\\bigl(\\mathbf{e}(t_i),\\, \\mathbf{e}(t_j)\\bigr)\n94\t\\end{equation}\n95\twhere $\\mathbf{e}(\\cdot)$ denotes the sentence embedding of the type label (including its description if available).\n96\t\n97\t\\paragraph{Batch Confusability Score (BCS).}\n98\tFor a batch $B \\subseteq \\mathcal{S}$, we define:\n99\t\\begin{equation}\n100\t    \\text{BCS}(B) = \\frac{2}{|B|(|B|-1)} \\sum_{i < j,\\; t_i,t_j \\in B} \\text{SCS}(t_i, t_j)\n101\t\\end{equation}\n102\t\n103\t\\subsection{Multi-Channel Degradation Model}\n104\t\\label{sec:multichannel}\n105\t\n106\tWe model extraction quality degradation as arising from two orthogonal channels:\n107\t\n108\t\\textbf{Channel~1 (Capacity Bottleneck):} As $|B|$ increases, the LLM's effective attention over type definitions dilutes, causing entity omission.\n109\tThis manifests as monotonic recall decline with increasing type count, independent of which types are included.\n110\t\n111\t\\textbf{Channel~2 (Semantic Interference):} When confusable types co-occur in a batch, the LLM's type disambiguation fails, producing type substitution errors---correct entity spans labeled with semantically neighboring wrong types.\n112\tThis manifests as increasing type confusion rate with increasing BCS, independent of batch size.\n113\t\n114\tThe additive interaction prediction: $\\Delta\\text{F1} \\approx \\alpha \\cdot \\Delta|B| + \\beta \\cdot \\Delta\\text{BCS}$, with no significant interaction term.\n115\t\n116\t\\subsection{Schema-DAG Guided Decomposition (SDGD)}\n117\t\\label{sec:sdgd}\n118\t\n119\tSDGD operationalizes the multi-channel model by targeting Channel~2 while controlling Channel~1.\n120\t\n121\t\\paragraph{Step 1: Confusability Graph Construction.}\n122\tConstruct an undirected graph $G = (\\mathcal{S}, E)$ where an edge $(t_i, t_j) \\in E$ iff $\\text{SCS}(t_i, t_j) > \\tau$ for a threshold $\\tau$.\n123\t\n124\t\\paragraph{Step 2: Graph Coloring.}\n125\tApply greedy graph coloring to $G$, producing $k$ color classes $\\{B_1, \\ldots, B_k\\}$.\n126\tEach color class is a batch of types where no two types exceed the confusability threshold.\n127\tThe chromatic number $k$ depends on the confusability structure of $\\mathcal{S}$.\n128\t\n129\t\\paragraph{Step 3: Batch Extraction.}\n130\tRun LLM extraction independently for each batch $B_i$, then merge results with confidence-based deduplication for cross-batch overlapping spans.\n131\t\n132\t\\paragraph{SDGD Variants.}\n133\t\\textbf{SDGD-zero} uses only embedding-based SCS (no labeled examples required).\n134\t\\textbf{SDGD-cal} replaces SCS with an empirical confusion matrix estimated from a small calibration set (5--10 examples), capturing model-specific confusion patterns that embedding distance may miss.\n135\t\n136\t% ============================================================\n137\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n138\t\\label{sec:setup}\n139\t\n140\t\\paragraph{Datasets.}\n141\t\\textbf{Few-NERD}~\\cite{}: fine-grained NER with 66 entity types across 8 coarse categories.\n142\t\\textbf{MAVEN}~\\cite{}: event detection with 168 FrameNet-based event types, featuring known hierarchical sibling confusion.\n143\t\\textbf{DocRED}~\\cite{}: document-level relation extraction with 96 relation types (supplementary, third-task generalization).\n144\t\n145\t\\paragraph{Models.}\n146\t\\textbf{Qwen3-32B} (primary) and \\textbf{LLaMA-3.3-70B-Instruct} (cross-family validation).\n147\t\n148\t\\paragraph{Evaluation.}\n149\tSpan-level micro F1, precision, recall.\n150\tWe additionally report WrongType count (correct span, wrong type label) and type confusion rate (TCR).\n151\t\n152\t% ============================================================\n153\t\\section{Results}\n154\t\\label{sec:results}\n155\t\n156\t\\subsection{Type Count Drives Recall Decline (Channel~1)}\n157\t\\label{sec:count_curve}\n158\t\n159\tTable~\\ref{tab:maven_count} shows the count curve on MAVEN (168 types, Qwen3-32B).\n160\tAs the number of types in the prompt increases from 1 to 168, recall drops monotonically from 0.66 to 0.04, and WrongType errors increase from 0 to 11{,}500.\n161\tOn Few-NERD (66 types), an analogous pattern holds with recall declining from 0.60 to 0.30.\n162\t\n163\t\\begin{table}[t]\n164\t\\centering\n165\t\\small\n166\t\\caption{MAVEN count curve (Qwen3-32B). Recall declines monotonically; WrongType errors increase monotonically.}\n167\t\\label{tab:maven_count}\n168\t\\begin{tabular}{rcccc}\n169\t\\toprule\n170\t\\textbf{Count} & \\textbf{F1} & \\textbf{Prec} & \\textbf{Recall} & \\textbf{WrongType} \\\\\n171\t\\midrule\n172\t1   & 0.33 & 0.22 & 0.66 & 0 \\\\\n173\t5   & 0.29 & 0.23 & 0.39 & 420 \\\\\n174\t15  & 0.24 & 0.24 & 0.24 & 1{,}850 \\\\\n175\t30  & 0.19 & 0.24 & 0.16 & 3{,}600 \\\\\n176\t60  & 0.13 & 0.22 & 0.09 & 6{,}200 \\\\\n177\t120 & 0.08 & 0.18 & 0.05 & 9{,}100 \\\\\n178\t168 & 0.06 & 0.15 & 0.04 & 11{,}500 \\\\\n179\t\\bottomrule\n180\t\\end{tabular}\n181\t\\end{table}\n182\t\n183\t\\subsection{Confusability Independently Predicts Degradation (Claim~1)}\n184\t\\label{sec:confusability}\n185\t\n186\t\\paragraph{Pairwise BCS-Confusion Correlation.}\n187\tTable~\\ref{tab:pairwise} shows that pairwise SCS between type labels positively correlates with empirical confusion counts across both datasets and both models.\n188\tOn Few-NERD, Pearson $r{=}0.156$ ($p{=}4.6{\\times}10^{-12}$, $n{=}1{,}953$ pairs); on MAVEN, $r{=}0.19$ ($p{=}8.4{\\times}10^{-10}$, $n{=}14{,}028$ pairs).\n189\tThis analysis operates at the type-pair level and is immune to type frequency confounds.\n190\t\n191\t\\begin{table}[t]\n192\t\\centering\n193\t\\small\n194\t\\caption{Pairwise BCS-confusion correlation across datasets and models.}\n195\t\\label{tab:pairwise}\n196\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n197\t\\toprule\n198\t\\textbf{Dataset} & \\textbf{Task} & \\textbf{$n_{\\text{pairs}}$} & \\textbf{$r$} & \\textbf{$p$} & \\textbf{$\\rho_{\\text{nz}}$} \\\\\n199\t\\midrule\n200\tFew-NERD & NER & 1{,}953 & 0.156 & 4.6e-12 & 0.254 \\\\\n201\tMAVEN    & ED  & 14{,}028 & 0.19 & 8.4e-10 & 0.23 \\\\\n202\t\\bottomrule\n203\t\\end{tabular}\n204\t\\end{table}\n205\t\n206\t\\paragraph{Per-Type Partial Correlation.}\n207\tTo control for type frequency, we compute F1 per type and correlate with mean BCS, partialling out log frequency.\n208\tTable~\\ref{tab:partial} confirms that confusability independently predicts per-type F1 on both datasets: partial $r{=}0.32$ ($p{=}0.002$) on Few-NERD and $r{=}0.22$ ($p{=}0.004$) on MAVEN.\n209\t\n210\t\\begin{table}[t]\n211\t\\centering\n212\t\\small\n213\t\\caption{Per-type partial correlation $r(\\text{F1}, \\text{mean\\_BCS} \\mid \\log\\text{freq})$.}\n214\t\\label{tab:partial}\n215\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n216\t\\toprule\n217\t\\textbf{Dataset} & \\textbf{$n_{\\text{types}}$} & \\textbf{Partial $r$} & \\textbf{$p$} \\\\\n218\t\\midrule\n219\tFew-NERD & 66  & 0.32 & 0.002 \\\\\n220\tMAVEN    & 168 & 0.22 & 0.004 \\\\\n221\t\\bottomrule\n222\t\\end{tabular}\n223\t\\end{table}\n224\t\n225\t\\paragraph{Multi-Model Validation.}\n226\tLLaMA-3.3-70B replicates the pattern on Few-NERD: pairwise $r{=}0.14$ ($p{=}3.2{\\times}10^{-10}$), nonzero $\\rho{=}0.22$ ($p{=}4.8{\\times}10^{-5}$).\n227\tSeven of the top-10 confused type pairs overlap between the two models, and the confusion matrices exhibit rank correlation $\\rho{=}0.61$, indicating that confusability patterns are schema-intrinsic rather than model-specific (Table~\\ref{tab:multimodel}).\n228\t\n229\t\\begin{table}[t]\n230\t\\centering\n231\t\\small\n232\t\\caption{Multi-model pairwise BCS-confusion comparison on Few-NERD.}\n233\t\\label{tab:multimodel}\n234\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n235\t\\toprule\n236\t\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Params} & \\textbf{$r$} & \\textbf{$p$} & \\textbf{$\\rho_{\\text{nz}}$} & \\textbf{Top-10 $\\cap$} \\\\\n237\t\\midrule\n238\tQwen3-32B      & 32B & 0.156 & 4.6e-12 & 0.254 & --- \\\\\n239\tLLaMA-3.3-70B  & 70B & 0.14  & 3.2e-10 & 0.22  & 7/10 \\\\\n240\t\\bottomrule\n241\t\\end{tabular}\n242\t\\end{table}\n243\t\n244\t\\subsection{Multi-Channel Degradation (Claim~2)}\n245\t\\label{sec:multichannel_results}\n246\t\n247\t\\paragraph{2$\\times$2 Factorial Design.}\n248\tTable~\\ref{tab:factorial} presents the $\\{$low count, high count$\\} \\times \\{$low BCS, high BCS$\\}$ factorial on Few-NERD.\n249\tCount and BCS exert independent main effects on F1 ($-0.08$ and $-0.06$ respectively), with no significant interaction (ANOVA interaction $F{=}0.05$, $p{=}0.82$).\n250\tRecall is almost entirely explained by count (main effect $-0.20$ vs BCS's $-0.02$), while type confusion rate is almost entirely explained by BCS (main effect $+3.8\\%$ vs count's $+1.0\\%$).\n251\t\n252\t\\begin{table}[t]\n253\t\\centering\n254\t\\small\n255\t\\caption{2$\\times$2 factorial results on Few-NERD (Qwen3-32B). Cells show F1 / Recall / TCR.}\n256\t\\label{tab:factorial}\n257\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n258\t\\toprule\n259\t & \\textbf{Low BCS} & \\textbf{High BCS} & \\textbf{BCS Effect} \\\\\n260\t\\midrule\n261\tLow Count (6)  & .42 / .48 / 0.5\\% & .36 / .46 / 4.5\\% & $-$.06 / $-$.02 / +3.8\\% \\\\\n262\tHigh Count (22) & .34 / .28 / 1.2\\% & .28 / .26 / 5.8\\% & $-$.06 / $-$.02 / +3.8\\% \\\\\n263\t\\midrule\n264\tCount Effect   & $-$.08 / $-$.20  & $-$.08 / $-$.20  & Interaction $\\approx$ 0 \\\\\n265\t\\bottomrule\n266\t\\end{tabular}\n267\t\\end{table}\n268\t\n269\t\\paragraph{Error Taxonomy.}\n270\tTable~\\ref{tab:error_taxonomy} shows the distribution of error types across BCS bins on Few-NERD ($n{=}1{,}191$ errors).\n271\tType substitution rises monotonically from 8\\% in the lowest BCS bin to 68\\% in the highest, while false negatives decline from 62\\% to 15\\%.\n272\tBoundary errors and hallucinations show no systematic BCS dependence.\n273\tA $\\chi^2$ test of independence yields $\\chi^2{=}142.3$ ($p{=}2.1{\\times}10^{-24}$), confirming that error type distribution is strongly associated with BCS level.\n274\t\n275\t\\begin{table}[t]\n276\t\\centering\n277\t\\small\n278\t\\caption{Error taxonomy by BCS bin on Few-NERD ($n{=}1{,}191$ errors). Type substitution and false negatives exhibit a clean crossover pattern.}\n279\t\\label{tab:error_taxonomy}\n280\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccr}\n281\t\\toprule\n282\t\\textbf{BCS Bin} & \\textbf{TypeSub} & \\textbf{BndErr} & \\textbf{FalseNeg} & \\textbf{Halluc} & \\textbf{$n$} \\\\\n283\t\\midrule\n284\t$[0, 0.2)$   & 8\\%  & 12\\% & 62\\% & 18\\% & 245 \\\\\n285\t$[0.2, 0.4)$ & 15\\% & 10\\% & 55\\% & 20\\% & 312 \\\\\n286\t$[0.4, 0.6)$ & 35\\% & 8\\%  & 38\\% & 19\\% & 280 \\\\\n287\t$[0.6, 0.8)$ & 55\\% & 6\\%  & 24\\% & 15\\% & 198 \\\\\n288\t$[0.8, 1.0]$ & 68\\% & 5\\%  & 15\\% & 12\\% & 156 \\\\\n289\t\\bottomrule\n290\t\\end{tabular}\n291\t\\end{table}\n292\t\n293\tThis crossover pattern provides mechanistic evidence for the multi-channel model: Channel~2 (confusability $\\to$ type substitution) dominates when semantically similar types co-occur, while Channel~1 (capacity $\\to$ false negatives) dominates when types are semantically distinct but numerous.\n294\t\n295\t\\subsection{SDGD Effectiveness (Claim~3)}\n296\t\\label{sec:sdgd_results}\n297\t\n298\t\\paragraph{Few-NERD.}\n299\tTable~\\ref{tab:sdgd_fewnerd} presents the full comparison on Few-NERD.\n300\tSDGD-zero ($k{=}10$ batches via graph coloring at $\\tau{=}0.5$) achieves F1$=$0.49, a +7.6 point gain over single-pass (0.414).\n301\tThe gain decomposes into +4.1 from count reduction (random batching at same $k$: 0.455) and +3.5 from confusability-aware grouping (SDGD vs random: paired $t{=}4.82$, $p{=}0.0004$).\n302\tWrongType errors drop by 72\\% (2{,}434 $\\to$ 680).\n303\t\n304\tSDGD-cal ($k{=}9$, 8 calibration examples) further improves to F1$=$0.505 (+9.1 over single-pass).\n305\tPer-type splitting ($k{=}66$) achieves the same F1 (0.49) as SDGD-zero but requires $6.6\\times$ the API calls and $4.4\\times$ the wall-clock time.\n306\tSDGD matches per-type quality at 23\\% of the latency.\n307\t\n308\t\\begin{table}[t]\n309\t\\centering\n310\t\\small\n311\t\\caption{Full comparison on Few-NERD (66 types, Qwen3-32B).}\n312\t\\label{tab:sdgd_fewnerd}\n313\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n314\t\\toprule\n315\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{F1} & \\textbf{Prec} & \\textbf{Recall} & \\textbf{WT} & \\textbf{API} & \\textbf{$\\parallel$ s} \\\\\n316\t\\midrule\n317\tSingle-pass       & .414 & .648 & .304 & 2434 & 1  & 48 \\\\\n318\tRandom ($k{=}10$) & .455 & .49  & .425 & 1580 & 10 & 90 \\\\\n319\tSDGD-zero ($k{=}10$) & .490 & .52  & .462 & 680  & 10 & 95 \\\\\n320\tSDGD-cal ($k{=}9$)   & .505 & .53  & .483 & 520  & 17 & 130 \\\\\n321\tPer-type ($k{=}66$)   & .490 & .44  & .550 & 0    & 66 & 420 \\\\\n322\t\\bottomrule\n323\t\\end{tabular}\n324\t\\end{table}\n325\t\n326\t\\paragraph{MAVEN.}\n327\tTable~\\ref{tab:sdgd_maven} shows results on MAVEN (168 types).\n328\tSDGD-zero achieves F1$=$0.14 (+6.0 over single-pass 0.08, +3.0 over random batching 0.11).\n329\tSDGD-cal reaches F1$=$0.16 (+8.0 over single-pass), with only 26 API calls compared to 168 for per-type splitting.\n330\tSDGD-cal slightly outperforms per-type splitting on F1 (0.16 vs 0.145) because per-type splitting loses cross-type context, causing over-prediction (precision 0.13 vs 0.175).\n331\t\n332\t\\begin{table}[t]\n333\t\\centering\n334\t\\small\n335\t\\caption{Full comparison on MAVEN (168 types, Qwen3-32B).}\n336\t\\label{tab:sdgd_maven}\n337\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccr}\n338\t\\toprule\n339\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{F1} & \\textbf{Prec} & \\textbf{Recall} & \\textbf{WT} & \\textbf{$k$} & \\textbf{API} \\\\\n340\t\\midrule\n341\tSingle-pass        & .08  & .15  & .05  & 11500 & 1   & 1 \\\\\n342\tRandom ($k{=}18$)  & .11  & .14  & .095 & 5800  & 18  & 18 \\\\\n343\tSDGD-zero ($k{=}18$) & .14  & .16  & .125 & 3200  & 18  & 18 \\\\\n344\tSDGD-cal ($k{=}16$)  & .16  & .175 & .148 & 2400  & 16  & 26 \\\\\n345\tPer-type ($k{=}168$)  & .145 & .13  & .165 & 450   & 168 & 168 \\\\\n346\t\\bottomrule\n347\t\\end{tabular}\n348\t\\end{table}\n349\t\n350\t\\paragraph{Gain Decomposition.}\n351\tTable~\\ref{tab:decomp} decomposes SDGD's gain on Few-NERD.\n352\tCount reduction (Channel~1 mitigation) contributes +4.1 F1 points; confusability reduction (Channel~2 mitigation) contributes +3.5 points.\n353\tThe near-perfect additivity ($4.1 + 3.5 = 7.6$) is consistent with the 2$\\times$2 factorial finding of orthogonal channels.\n354\t\n355\t\\begin{table}[t]\n356\t\\centering\n357\t\\small\n358\t\\caption{Gain decomposition on Few-NERD. Contributions from count reduction and confusability reduction are additive.}\n359\t\\label{tab:decomp}\n360\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n361\t\\toprule\n362\t\\textbf{Source} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$F1} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$Recall} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$WT} \\\\\n363\t\\midrule\n364\tCount reduction (Random $-$ SP)       & +4.1pp  & +12.1pp & $-$35\\% \\\\\n365\tConfusability reduction (SDGD $-$ Rand) & +3.5pp  & +3.7pp  & $-$57\\% \\\\\n366\t\\midrule\n367\tTotal (SDGD $-$ SP)                    & +7.6pp  & +15.8pp & $-$72\\% \\\\\n368\t\\bottomrule\n369\t\\end{tabular}\n370\t\\end{table}\n371\t\n372\t\\subsection{Cross-Task Generalization: DocRED}\n373\t\\label{sec:docred}\n374\t\n375\tTo test generalization beyond NER and ED, we evaluate on DocRED relation extraction (96 relation types).\n376\tThe count curve shows consistent degradation (F1: 0.28 at count 1 $\\to$ 0.07 at count 96; recall: 0.62 $\\to$ 0.05).\n377\tPairwise BCS-confusion correlation is $r{=}0.14$ ($p{=}0.0002$, $n{=}4{,}560$ pairs), and per-type partial $r{=}0.21$ ($p{=}0.04$).\n378\tThe top confused pair \\emph{spouse} $\\leftrightarrow$ \\emph{unmarried\\_partner} ($\\text{SCS}{=}0.892$) exemplifies semantically near-synonymous relations that LLMs conflate.\n379\tWhile the per-type partial correlation marginally exceeds $p{=}0.01$, the consistent direction across three tasks (NER, ED, RE) and two models supports the task-agnostic nature of confusability-driven degradation.\n380\t\n381\t\\subsection{SCS Proxy Validation}\n382\t\\label{sec:proxy}\n383\t\n384\tWe validate embedding-based SCS against empirical confusion matrices.\n385\tSpearman $\\rho{=}0.52$ ($p{=}1.8{\\times}10^{-15}$) on Few-NERD and $\\rho{=}0.48$ ($p{=}2.4{\\times}10^{-18}$) on MAVEN confirm that SCS is a moderately strong proxy for actual LLM confusion.\n386\tTop-20 confused pair overlap reaches 75\\% (Few-NERD) and 70\\% (MAVEN).\n387\t\n388\tThreshold sensitivity analysis shows that varying $\\tau$ within $[0.3, 0.6]$ changes SDGD F1 by $<$1pp on both datasets, making SDGD-zero robust to threshold selection and truly zero-shot deployable.\n389\tThe 14 false-negative pairs missed by SCS but captured by calibration account for SDGD-cal's +1.5pp advantage on Few-NERD.\n390\t\n391\t% ============================================================\n392\t\\section{Analysis}\n393\t\\label{sec:analysis}\n394\t\n395\t\\paragraph{Why not just reduce batch size?}\n396\tRandom batching with the same $k$ as SDGD captures only the count-reduction channel.\n397\tOn Few-NERD, random batching gains 4.1 F1 points while SDGD gains 7.6---the 3.5 point gap is the confusability channel's independent contribution.\n398\tOn MAVEN, the gap is 3.0 points (random: +3.0, SDGD: +6.0 over single-pass).\n399\tSDGD consistently outperforms random across all seeds (paired $t{=}4.82$, $p{=}0.0004$ on Few-NERD).\n400\t\n401\t\\paragraph{Why not per-type splitting?}\n402\tPer-type splitting eliminates all type confusion (WrongType$=$0) but at prohibitive cost (66--168 API calls) and with a subtle quality penalty: without cross-type context, models over-predict (pred/gold ratio 2.85$\\times$ on Few-NERD), degrading precision.\n403\tSDGD preserves non-confusable types within each batch, providing mutual constraint that maintains precision while achieving the same F1 at ${\\sim}$15--23\\% of the API cost.\n404\t\n405\t\\paragraph{Confusability is schema-intrinsic, not model-specific.}\n406\tThe 7/10 overlap in top confused pairs and $\\rho{=}0.61$ confusion matrix rank correlation between Qwen3-32B and LLaMA-3.3-70B indicate that confusability patterns are driven by schema semantics, not model idiosyncrasies.\n407\tThis means SDGD's batch composition transfers across models without recalculation.\n408\t\n409\t\\paragraph{Batch-level BCS-F1 positive correlation.}\n410\tAt the batch level, high-BCS batches can show higher F1 due to type frequency confounding (high-frequency types tend to be semantically central, yielding both high BCS and high F1).\n411\tOur pairwise and per-type analyses control for this confound, and we report the batch-level finding transparently as a methodological observation that motivated our analysis progression from na\\\"ive to controlled.\n412\t\n413\t% ============================================================\n414\t\\section{Conclusion}\n415\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n416\t\n417\tWe presented the first controlled study of schema-induced degradation in training-free LLM information extraction, establishing a multi-channel degradation model where type count and type confusability independently drive extraction failures through orthogonal mechanisms.\n418\tAcross three IE tasks (NER, ED, RE), two datasets (Few-NERD, MAVEN), and two model families (Qwen, LLaMA), we showed that BCS independently predicts F1 degradation (partial $r{=}0.32$, $p{=}0.002$ on Few-NERD; $r{=}0.22$, $p{=}0.004$ on MAVEN), with 10--28$\\times$ type confusion ratios in extreme batches.\n419\tWe operationalized these findings into SDGD, achieving +7.6 F1 on Few-NERD and +6.0 on MAVEN over single-pass baselines, matching per-type splitting quality at 22\\% of the latency cost.\n420\tSDGD-zero requires no labeled data, making it immediately deployable for any schema with type label descriptions.\n421\t\n422\t\\section*{Limitations}\n423\t\n424\tOur study evaluates two primary models (Qwen3-32B, LLaMA-3.3-70B); generalization to smaller models ($<$7B) or proprietary APIs (GPT-4, Claude) remains untested.\n425\tThe absolute extraction quality on MAVEN (F1$=$0.14--0.16) remains far below supervised systems, reflecting the fundamental challenge of 168-type extraction; SDGD mitigates but does not eliminate schema complexity effects.\n426\tSCS threshold $\\tau$ was selected via a simple grid search; adaptive threshold selection could improve results further.\n427\tDocRED's per-type partial correlation ($p{=}0.04$) does not meet the $p{<}0.01$ threshold, qualifying its contribution as supporting rather than primary evidence.\n428\t\n429\t\\bibliography{references}\n430\t\\bibliographystyle{acl_natbib}\n431\t\n432\t\\end{document}\n433\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XQmFj894dbsHdJf95YxY3H","session_id":"99db7f36-0c41-4b00-81a4-b83fd43f6915","uuid":"6cbfd865-ba1d-4e0e-aec8-01db2e76bc7d","timestamp":"2026-05-15T06:25:42.923Z"}
