{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PM7oasEGdS47xNJ2UjpV8K","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n6\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n7\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n8\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n12\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n13\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n14\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n15\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n16\t\n17\t\\title{Multi-Channel Schema Degradation in Training-Free LLM Information Extraction:\\\\ Isolating Confusability from Count}\n18\t\n19\t\\author{Anonymous}\n20\t\n21\t\\date{}\n22\t\n23\t\\begin{document}\n24\t\\maketitle\n25\t\n26\t\\begin{abstract}\n27\tTraining-free LLM information extraction degrades severely on complex schemas, yet the mechanisms behind this degradation remain poorly understood. We conduct the first controlled empirical study isolating \\emph{type confusability} from \\emph{type count} as independent drivers of extraction failure. Through experiments on two IE tasks---NER (Few-NERD, 66 types) and Event Detection (MAVEN, 168 types)---with two LLM families, we discover a multi-channel degradation model: type count drives recall decline through attention dilution (Channel~1), while type confusability drives type substitution errors through disambiguation failure (Channel~2). These channels are largely additive (interaction $F=2.3$, $p=0.135$). At the type-pair level, embedding-based confusability significantly predicts confusion frequency (Few-NERD $r=0.156$, $p<10^{-11}$; MAVEN $r=0.09$, $p=0.003$), and high-confusability batches exhibit $2.8$--$3.2\\times$ more type confusion than low-confusability batches. We operationalize these findings into Schema-DAG-Guided Decomposition (SDGD), a confusability-minimizing batch composition method using graph coloring on the type similarity graph. SDGD improves F1 by $+2.3$ on Few-NERD and $+4.0$ on MAVEN over single-pass baselines, achieving 93--99\\% of per-type splitting quality at 18--20\\% of the API cost.\n28\t\\end{abstract}\n29\t\n30\t%==============================================================================\n31\t\\section{Introduction}\n32\t\\label{sec:intro}\n33\t%==============================================================================\n34\t\n35\tTraining-free large language model (LLM) information extraction---where an LLM performs named entity recognition, event detection, or relation extraction using only a schema definition in the prompt, without task-specific training---has emerged as a practical paradigm for deployment scenarios where labeled data is unavailable \\cite{li2023evaluating,wang2023gptner}. Clinical NER, regulatory compliance extraction, and multilingual IE across 50+ languages routinely face this constraint.\n36\t\n37\tHowever, performance degrades severely as schema complexity increases. A 66-type NER schema yields F1 scores 20--40 points below supervised systems, and 168-type event schemas fare even worse. Prior work has attempted to address this through schema decomposition: UniNER \\cite{zhou2024universalner} splits per type, LLM-PEE uses similarity-based partitioning, and ProUIE employs fixed task-level decomposition. Yet all these approaches operate on unvalidated assumptions about \\emph{what makes schemas complex for LLMs}.\n38\t\n39\tWe argue that schema-induced degradation is not a monolithic phenomenon but a \\emph{multi-channel process}. Specifically, we hypothesize two orthogonal channels:\n40\t\\begin{itemize}\n41\t    \\item \\textbf{Channel 1 (Count $\\to$ Recall)}: More types in the prompt cause attention dilution, leading to missed entities.\n42\t    \\item \\textbf{Channel 2 (Confusability $\\to$ Type Confusion)}: Semantically similar co-present types cause disambiguation failure, leading to correct spans assigned wrong types.\n43\t\\end{itemize}\n44\t\n45\tTo test this hypothesis, we design controlled experiments that isolate confusability from count. We construct type batches of fixed size but varying confusability, measured by Schema Confusability Score (SCS)---the cosine similarity between type name embeddings. We validate across two IE tasks (NER on Few-NERD, Event Detection on MAVEN), two model families (Qwen3-32B, LLaMA-3.3-70B), and multiple analysis granularities (batch-level, per-type, and pairwise).\n46\t\n47\tOur contributions are:\n48\t\\begin{enumerate}\n49\t    \\item \\textbf{Confusability as independent predictor}: We show that type-pair confusability contributes to prediction of extraction degradation beyond count, with strongest evidence at the type-pair mechanistic level (pairwise $r=0.156$, $p<10^{-11}$ on Few-NERD; $r=0.09$, $p=0.003$ on MAVEN), replicated across models and tasks.\n50\t    \\item \\textbf{Multi-channel degradation model}: We demonstrate that count and confusability degrade extraction through largely additive channels ($p_{\\text{interaction}}=0.135$), with a clear error taxonomy: high-confusability batches show 47\\% type substitution errors vs.\\ 18\\% in low-confusability batches ($\\chi^2$ $p=0.001$).\n51\t    \\item \\textbf{SDGD framework}: We operationalize confusability-aware decomposition via graph coloring, achieving significant gains on MAVEN ($+4.0$ F1, $p=0.002$) and matching per-type splitting quality at $\\sim$20\\% of the cost.\n52\t    \\item \\textbf{Methodological contribution}: We document the discovery and resolution of a type frequency confound in batch-level analysis, demonstrating the necessity of pairwise and per-type analysis granularities.\n53\t\\end{enumerate}\n54\t\n55\t%==============================================================================\n56\t\\section{Related Work}\n57\t\\label{sec:related}\n58\t%==============================================================================\n59\t\n60\t\\paragraph{Training-free LLM IE.}\n61\tGPT-NER \\cite{wang2023gptner} and similar systems prompt LLMs with type definitions for zero-shot extraction. Performance degrades with schema size, but the mechanism is unstudied. UniNER \\cite{zhou2024universalner} addresses this by per-type splitting but sacrifices cross-type context and provides no criterion for when decomposition is beneficial.\n62\t\n63\t\\paragraph{Schema decomposition.}\n64\tLLM-PEE uses embedding similarity to partition types, implicitly targeting confusability without validation. ProUIE and KGGen employ fixed task-level decomposition that ignores intra-layer schema structure. None of these approaches isolate confusability from count or validate their decomposition criterion.\n65\t\n66\t\\paragraph{Prompt complexity and LLM performance.}\n67\t``Lost in the Middle'' \\cite{liu2024lost} demonstrates that LLMs struggle with information positioned in the middle of long contexts. Our work extends this to structured schema definitions, showing that degradation depends not just on schema length but on inter-type semantic relationships.\n68\t\n69\t%==============================================================================\n70\t\\section{Method}\n71\t\\label{sec:method}\n72\t%==============================================================================\n73\t\n74\t\\subsection{Problem Setting}\n75\t\n76\tWe study training-free LLM IE: given a document $d$ and a type schema $\\mathcal{T} = \\{t_1, \\ldots, t_n\\}$ with natural-language definitions, an LLM extracts typed mentions $\\{(s_i, t_i)\\}$ from $d$ without any task-specific training data.\n77\t\n78\t\\subsection{Schema Confusability Score (SCS)}\n79\t\n80\tFor each type pair $(t_i, t_j)$, we compute the \\emph{Schema Confusability Score}:\n81\t\\begin{equation}\n82\t    \\text{SCS}(t_i, t_j) = \\cos\\bigl(\\mathbf{e}(t_i),\\; \\mathbf{e}(t_j)\\bigr)\n83\t\\end{equation}\n84\twhere $\\mathbf{e}(t)$ is the embedding of type $t$'s name. For a batch $B \\subseteq \\mathcal{T}$, the \\emph{Batch Confusability Score} is:\n85\t\\begin{equation}\n86\t    \\text{BCS}(B) = \\max_{t_i, t_j \\in B,\\; i \\neq j} \\text{SCS}(t_i, t_j)\n87\t\\end{equation}\n88\t\n89\tWe validate SCS as a proxy for empirical confusion using calibration data (\\S\\ref{sec:scs_validation}). The overall rank correlation between SCS and empirical confusion frequency is $\\rho=0.38$ ($p<10^{-5}$), confirming SCS is a useful but imperfect proxy.\n90\t\n91\t\\subsection{Controlled Batch Construction}\n92\t\n93\tTo isolate confusability from count, we construct type batches with a $\\text{count} \\times \\text{BCS}$ factorial design. For each count level $c \\in \\{1, 6, 12, 22, \\ldots\\}$, we create batches of size $c$ from $\\mathcal{T}$. Within each count level, we vary BCS by selecting either semantically distant types (low BCS) or semantically similar types (high BCS).\n94\t\n95\t\\subsection{SDGD: Schema-DAG-Guided Decomposition}\n96\t\\label{sec:sdgd}\n97\t\n98\tSDGD partitions $\\mathcal{T}$ into $k$ batches that minimize within-batch confusability:\n99\t\n100\t\\begin{enumerate}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01NAeqckno26Vy8ftZqspmiN","session_id":"99db7f36-0c41-4b00-81a4-b83fd43f6915","uuid":"c313313b-3832-495d-bd6c-60058a2e763a","timestamp":"2026-05-15T06:25:50.101Z"}
