{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XqE3woQEMdx5wdC9CSaCph","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260515T053106\n3\ttier: median\n4\toverall_score: weak accept — EMNLP Findings recommended, not main conference in current form\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-15T06:23:52.638049+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Dream Review Report: Multi-Channel Schema Degradation in Training-Free LLM IE\n9\t\n10\t**dream_id**: 20260515T053106  \n11\t**Review mode**: full (2 Reviewers + Roundtable)  \n12\t**Date**: 2026-05-15\n13\t\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t## Paper Summary\n17\t\n18\tThe paper investigates why training-free LLM information extraction degrades on complex schemas. It proposes a two-channel degradation model: type count drives recall decline via attention dilution (Channel 1), while type confusability drives type substitution errors via disambiguation failure (Channel 2). Through controlled experiments on Few-NERD (66 NER types) and MAVEN (168 event types) with two LLM families (Qwen3-32B, LLaMA-3.3-70B), the paper demonstrates these channels are largely additive (interaction F=2.3, p=0.135). The findings are operationalized into SDGD (Schema-DAG-Guided Decomposition), a graph-coloring-based batch composition method that improves F1 by +2.3 on Few-NERD and +4.0 on MAVEN over single-pass baselines, achieving 93–99% of per-type splitting quality at 18–20% of the API cost.\n19\t\n20\t---\n21\t\n22\t## Reviewer R1: Methods and Experiments\n23\t\n24\t### Strengths\n25\t\n26\t**S1. Rigorous factorial design.** The 2×2 factorial (count × BCS) with ANOVA is the correct methodology for the additivity claim. Both main effects are significant (count F=8.2, p=0.006; BCS F=5.1, p=0.028) while the interaction is not (F=2.3, p=0.135). The channel decomposition is clean: count primarily affects recall (Δ=−0.160), BCS primarily affects precision (Δ=−0.081).\n27\t\n28\t**S2. Error taxonomy provides mechanistic evidence beyond correlation.** The monotonic crossover — type substitution rising from 18% to 52% with BCS while false negatives fall from 55% to 28% (χ²=18.4, p=0.001) — moves the argument from correlation to identifiable error modes. This is considerably more informative than correlation alone.\n29\t\n30\t**S3. Transparent handling of frequency confound.** The discovery that batch-level BCS–F1 correlation is positive (opposite to hypothesis) and the subsequent pivot to pairwise analysis is commendably reported. This kind of methodological honesty strengthens credibility and constitutes an independent contribution.\n31\t\n32\t**S4. Comprehensive ablation structure.** Random batching (same k) is the correct control for isolating confusability-aware grouping from count reduction. Per-type splitting establishes the quality upper bound. The decomposition of SDGD gains into Channel 1 (batch reduction) and Channel 2 (confusability-aware grouping) components is well-designed.\n33\t\n34\t### Weaknesses\n35\t\n36\t**W1 (Major). Missing head-to-head with LLM-PEE.** LLM-PEE already performs embedding-similarity-based type partitioning for LLM IE. Without a direct comparison on the same datasets, it is impossible to assess SDGD's marginal contribution over existing methods. This is the most critical missing baseline.\n37\t\n38\t**W2 (Major). Small pairwise effect sizes with significance inflated by large n.** Pairwise r=0.156 (Few-NERD) and r=0.09 (MAVEN) explain only 2.4% and 0.8% of variance respectively. With n=1,953 and n=14,028 pairs, trivial correlations achieve extreme significance. The paper should report R² and discuss what explains the remaining 97%+ of confusion variance. The nonzero-subset correlations (ρ=0.254 Few-NERD, ρ=0.15 MAVEN) and per-type partial correlations are stronger but still modest.\n39\t\n40\t**W3 (Major). BCS definition inconsistency.** Equation 2 defines BCS as max pairwise SCS within a batch, but different analyses appear to use max vs. mean interchangeably. The paper should clarify which operationalization is used in each analysis.\n41\t\n42\t**W4 (Minor-Major). No variance reporting for SDGD results.** SDGD results appear to be single-run point estimates. Random baseline reports std across 5 seeds, but no confidence intervals for SDGD-zero or SDGD-cal. Without variance estimates, the practical significance of small gaps (e.g., SDGD-zero vs random +1.4 on Few-NERD, p=0.08) is hard to assess.\n43\t\n44\t**W5 (Minor). Low absolute performance limits practical claims.** MAVEN F1=0.135–0.192 even with SDGD-cal is far below practical utility. The paper should position SDGD as proof-of-concept rather than production-ready.\n45\t\n46\t**W6 (Minor). Only 2 LLMs tested.** Both Qwen3-32B and LLaMA-3.3-70B are dense open-weight transformers. No proprietary APIs or architecturally diverse models tested.\n47\t\n48\t**W7 (Minor). WrongType counts not normalized.** Absolute WrongType counts naturally increase with type count. Normalization by number of type pairs or predictions is needed.\n49\t\n50\t**W8 (Minor). Incomplete confound controls.** Controls for type frequency but not type label length, hierarchical depth, or entity base rate. BCS likely correlates with hierarchical proximity in Few-NERD.\n51\t\n52\t### Score: 4 (Weak Accept) | Confidence: 4/5\n53\t\n54\t---\n55\t\n56\t## Reviewer R2: Novelty and Related Work\n57\t\n58\t### Strengths\n59\t\n60\t**S1. The controlled disentanglement is a genuine contribution.** While \"more classes = harder\" and \"similar classes confuse each other\" are individually unsurprising, no prior work has rigorously factored these apart in training-free LLM IE with controlled experiments. The 2×2 factorial with near-zero interaction and the error taxonomy crossover move beyond anecdotal observation to quantified mechanistic evidence. This is the paper's core novelty and it is real.\n61\t\n62\t**S2. Cross-task, cross-model replication.** Demonstrating consistent confusability effects across NER (Few-NERD) and ED (MAVEN), and across two model families with the same top confused pairs, supports the claim that confusability is schema-intrinsic rather than model-specific. This breadth is above average for an empirical IE paper.\n63\t\n64\t**S3. Cost-quality tradeoff is practically meaningful.** SDGD-cal matching per-type splitting quality (0.192 vs 0.195 on MAVEN, p=0.82) at 14% of API cost is a concrete, deployable advantage. The threshold robustness across τ ∈ [0.3, 0.6] makes SDGD-zero genuinely zero-shot deployable.\n65\t\n66\t**S4. Well-structured evidence hierarchy.** The three-layer progression — pairwise correlation → per-type partial correlation → error taxonomy — builds from statistical association to mechanistic explanation, with each layer addressing confounds from the previous.\n67\t\n68\t### Weaknesses\n69\t\n70\t**W1 (Major). Missing LLM-PEE comparison.** LLM-PEE uses embedding-similarity-based partitioning for large-schema LLM event extraction. SDGD-zero also uses embedding similarity via graph coloring. Without running both on the same datasets, the incremental contribution of graph coloring over similarity-based recall partitioning is unverifiable. This is the single most damaging omission.\n71\t\n72\t**W2 (Major). Novelty framing is overstated.** The \"multi-channel degradation model\" label implies a formal computational model; what exists is an empirical observation that two known factors affect different error types. The word \"model\" should be \"framework\" or \"empirical account.\" The paper should clearly state it provides the first *controlled empirical demonstration* rather than implying theoretical novelty.\n73\t\n74\t**W3 (Major). Related work has significant gaps.** Missing references include:\n75\t- **CROSSAGENTIE** (ACL 2025 Findings): Multi-agent cross-type debate targeting type confusion — the closest conceptual competitor for Channel 2 mitigation.\n76\t- **IEPile** (ACL 2024): Hard-negative schema construction during training — the training-time analog of confusability-aware decomposition.\n77\t- **GLiNER/GLiNER2**: Non-LLM competitive baselines for zero-shot NER handling large type inventories through parallel extraction.\n78\t- **PARSE** (EMNLP 2025 Industry): LLM-driven schema rewriting as alternative schema optimization.\n79\t\n80\tThe Related Work section (3 short paragraphs) is thin and misses the multi-agent and schema-optimization threads.\n81\t\n82\t**W4 (Major). Small effect sizes need honest scoping.** r=0.156 explains 2.4% of variance. The paper relies on large n for significance but should report R² and discuss the gap between \"statistically significant independent predictor\" and \"strong predictor.\" The SDGD results partially compensate, but the analytical claims need tighter scoping.\n83\t\n84\t**W5 (Minor). SCS is simplistic.** Cosine similarity of type name embeddings ignores type definitions, hierarchical relationships, and contextual usage. The SCS validation (ρ=0.38) shows moderate proxy quality. The paper should discuss when SCS fails systematically and whether type descriptions should be incorporated.\n85\t\n86\t**W6 (Minor). \"DAG\" misnomer in title.** The confusability graph is undirected; graph coloring operates on undirected graphs. Nothing in the method is a DAG. Title should be corrected.\n87\t\n88\t**W7 (Minor). Broken citations.** All references render as [?]. At any venue this risks desk rejection and prevents verifying attribution accuracy.\n89\t\n90\t**W8 (Minor). \"First controlled study\" needs qualification.** Should be narrowed to \"first controlled study isolating confusability from count in training-free, inference-time LLM IE.\"\n91\t\n92\t### Score: 4 (Weak Accept) | Confidence: 4/5\n93\t\n94\t---\n95\t\n96\t## Roundtable Discussion\n97\t\n98\t### Step 2: Cross-Sharing\n99\t\n100\tR1's methodological concerns (BCS definition inconsistency, WrongType normalization, incomplete confound controls) complement R2's broader positioning critique — together they show the paper has both internal clarity issues and external positioning gaps.\n101\t\n102\tR2's related work gaps (CROSSAGENTIE, IEPile, GLiNER) strengthen R1's LLM-PEE concern: it's not one missing baseline but a pattern of incomplete positioning against the schema-optimization literature.\n103\t\n104\tBoth reviewers independently identified the same top strengths (factorial design, error taxonomy, transparent confound handling) and same top weakness (missing LLM-PEE), lending confidence to the assessment.\n105\t\n106\t### Step 3: Convergence\n107\t\n108\t**Full agreement on:**\n109\t1. Score: 4 (Weak Accept) — both reviewers\n110\t2. Core contribution (factorial + error taxonomy) is genuine and well-executed\n111\t3. Missing LLM-PEE is the #1 weakness\n112\t4. Small pairwise effect sizes need R² reporting and honest discussion\n113\t5. The paper sits at the main conference / Findings boundary\n114\t6. \"DAG\" in title is a misnomer\n115\t7. Broken citations are a submission-quality issue\n116\t\n117\t**R1 uniquely contributed:** BCS definition inconsistency (max vs mean), variance reporting gap for SDGD, WrongType normalization issue, confound controls beyond frequency.\n118\t\n119\t**R2 uniquely contributed:** Related work gaps (4 specific missing references), novelty framing critique (\"model\" → \"framework\"), \"first controlled study\" qualification, SCS simplicity discussion.\n120\t\n121\t**No substantive disagreements required resolution.**\n122\t\n123\t### Step 4: Consolidated Assessment\n124\t\n125\t---\n126\t\n127\t## Final Consolidated Review\n128\t\n129\t### Overall Score: 4 (Weak Accept)\n130\t\n131\t### Confidence: 4/5\n132\t\n133\t### Decision Recommendation: EMNLP Findings (not main conference in current form)\n134\t\n135\t### Summary Assessment\n136\t\n137\tThe paper asks an important and well-scoped question — what makes complex schemas hard for training-free LLMs? — and answers it with above-average methodological rigor. The controlled 2×2 factorial design demonstrating additive channels, the error taxonomy showing mechanistic crossover between type substitution and false negatives, and the transparent handling of the frequency confound are genuine contributions that advance understanding of training-free IE. SDGD's cost-quality tradeoff (93–99% of per-type quality at 18–20% cost) is practically meaningful.\n138\t\n139\tHowever, the paper has three clusters of issues that prevent a main-conference recommendation:\n140\t\n141\t**1. Incomplete baselines (Critical).** The missing LLM-PEE comparison is the single most important gap. Both SDGD-zero and LLM-PEE use embedding similarity to partition types — without a head-to-head comparison, SDGD's incremental contribution over existing methods is unverifiable.\n142\t\n143\t**2. Effect size honesty (Major).** Pairwise correlations of r=0.09–0.156 explain <3% of confusion variance. The paper leverages large n for extreme p-values but should report R², discuss what explains the other 97%, and scope the \"independent predictor\" claim accordingly. The SDGD results provide practical validation, but the analytical narrative oversells the predictive power of SCS.\n144\t\n145\t**3. Positioning and presentation (Major).** The Related Work section is thin and misses several directly relevant works (CROSSAGENTIE, IEPile, GLiNER, PARSE). The \"multi-channel model\" framing overpromises theoretical novelty for what is an empirical framework. Broken citations ([?] throughout) are a submission-quality issue. \"DAG\" in the title is a misnomer.\n146\t\n147\t### Strengths (Consolidated, Ranked)\n148\t\n149\t1. **Rigorous factorial design with clean channel separation** — The 2×2 ANOVA with non-significant interaction (p=0.135) and clear channel decomposition (count→recall, BCS→precision) is the correct methodology and produces convincing evidence.\n150\t2. **Error taxonomy provides mechanistic evidence** — Type substitution 18%→52% and false negative 55%→28% across BCS bins (χ²=18.4, p=0.001) is more informative than correlation alone.\n151\t3. **Transparent frequency confound handling** — Reporting the contradictory batch-level result and pivoting to pairwise analysis is unusually honest scientific practice.\n152\t4. **Cross-task, cross-model replication** — Consistent direction across NER/ED and Qwen/LLaMA, with identical top confused pairs, supports schema-intrinsic claims.\n153\t5. **Comprehensive ablation decomposing SDGD gains** — Random batching control correctly isolates Channel 2 contribution. Per-type splitting establishes upper bound.\n154\t6. **Cost-quality tradeoff is concrete** — SDGD-cal at 14% of per-type API cost with matched F1 is deployable.\n155\t\n156\t### Weaknesses (Consolidated, Ranked)\n157\t\n158\t1. **(Critical) Missing LLM-PEE baseline** — Cannot assess SDGD's incremental value over existing embedding-based partitioning.\n159\t2. **(Major) Small pairwise effect sizes (r=0.09–0.156, R²<3%)** — Significance is driven by large n; practical predictive power of SCS is modest. Per-type partial correlations are marginal (p=0.065–0.11).\n160\t3. **(Major) Related work gaps** — CROSSAGENTIE, IEPile, GLiNER, PARSE are all directly relevant and unmentioned.\n161\t4. **(Major) Novelty framing overstated** — \"Multi-channel degradation model\" implies formal theory; the contribution is an empirical demonstration/framework.\n162\t5. **(Major) BCS definition inconsistency** — Max (Eq. 2) vs. mean used interchangeably across analyses without clarification.\n163\t6. **(Minor-Major) No variance/CI for SDGD results** — Single-run point estimates; key comparisons (SDGD-zero vs random on Few-NERD, p=0.08) are marginal.\n164\t7. **(Minor) Low absolute F1** — MAVEN 0.135–0.192 is far from practical utility; should frame as proof-of-concept.\n165\t8. **(Minor) Broken citations** — All references render as [?]; risks desk rejection.\n166\t9. **(Minor) \"DAG\" misnomer** — The confusability graph is undirected; graph coloring is not DAG traversal.\n167\t10. **(Minor) SCS uses only type names** — Ignoring type definitions and hierarchical structure is a missed opportunity; should discuss.\n168\t\n169\t### Questions for Authors\n170\t\n171\t1. Can you run LLM-PEE's partitioning algorithm on Few-NERD and MAVEN and report F1 alongside SDGD? This is essential for assessing incremental contribution.\n172\t2. What sentence encoder produces the SCS embeddings? This is absent and critical for reproducibility.\n173\t3. Can you report R² for all pairwise correlations and discuss what other factors explain the remaining 97%+ of confusion variance?\n174\t4. Is BCS defined as max or mean pairwise SCS? Which operationalization is used in each specific analysis?\n175\t5. Have you tested whether incorporating type descriptions (not just names) into SCS improves proxy quality?\n176\t6. How do you position against CROSSAGENTIE's reactive cross-type debate approach to type confusion?\n177\t7. Have you checked whether controlling for hierarchical depth changes the per-type partial correlation on Few-NERD?\n178\t\n179\t### Experiment Coverage Assessment\n180\t\n181\tRelative to the full experiment plan (11 experiments), the paper covers all planned experiments:\n182\t- ✅ MAVEN confusability study (exp-004): Fully reported\n183\t- ✅ DocRED study (exp-005): Reported as boundary condition (§9) — appropriate scoping\n184\t- ✅ 2×2 interaction (exp-006): Fully reported with ANOVA\n185\t- ✅ Error taxonomy (exp-007): Fully reported\n186\t- ✅ Multi-model validation (exp-012): Reported in §5.4\n187\t- ✅ SDGD-zero on Few-NERD (exp-008) and MAVEN (exp-009): Table 8\n188\t- ✅ SDGD-cal on Few-NERD (exp-010) and MAVEN (exp-011): Table 11\n189\t- ✅ Random batching ablation (exp-013): Table 10\n190\t- ✅ Per-type splitting (exp-014): Table 8\n191\t- ✅ SCS validation (exp-015): Table 12\n192\t\n193\tCoverage is comprehensive. The main gap is not in what experiments were run but in what baselines were compared against (LLM-PEE).\n194\t\n195\t### Actionable Revision Suggestions\n196\t\n197\t1. **Add LLM-PEE comparison** on at least MAVEN (where SDGD gains are largest).\n198\t2. **Report R²** alongside r for all pairwise correlations; add a paragraph discussing explained variance.\n199\t3. **Expand Related Work** to cover CROSSAGENTIE, IEPile, GLiNER, and PARSE.\n200\t4. **Rename \"model\" → \"framework\"** throughout when referring to the multi-channel account.\n201\t5. **Fix citations** — all render as [?].\n202\t6. **Clarify BCS operationalization** (max vs mean) in each analysis.\n203\t7. **Add confidence intervals** for SDGD results, or at minimum run 3 seeds.\n204\t8. **Correct \"DAG\"** in title — consider \"Schema-Graph-Guided Decomposition.\"\n205\t9. **Qualify \"first controlled study\"** to scope within training-free inference-time LLM IE.\n206\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01VtHaaJrH1gouPjpSidGSLX","session_id":"99db7f36-0c41-4b00-81a4-b83fd43f6915","uuid":"7627f1b5-5cba-4a52-8e88-65d2be14edd6","timestamp":"2026-05-15T06:28:27.377Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a517de810ebefcf91","tool_use_id":"toolu_01VtHaaJrH1gouPjpSidGSLX","description":"Reading artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T053106/median/paper.tex","usage":{"total_tokens":29412,"tool_uses":8,"duration_ms":19450},"last_tool_name":"Read","uuid":"fd5811dc-1754-4de2-a900-ee7ef7367e3c","session_id":"99db7f36-0c41-4b00-81a4-b83fd43f6915"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014dukYDkMgYAvdQuuci7DVD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UnJnNiwhH1BpXCjBd9Tvyj","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_18/projects/schema-dag-extraction/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T053106/median/paper.tex","offset":200,"limit":300},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10317,"cache_read_input_tokens":18780,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10317,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":38,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01VtHaaJrH1gouPjpSidGSLX","session_id":"99db7f36-0c41-4b00-81a4-b83fd43f6915","uuid":"df44a34f-dd93-4f29-acfc-ce3f8768b2e8"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UnJnNiwhH1BpXCjBd9Tvyj","type":"tool_result","content":"200\t28 & 0.038 & 0.0136 & 2.8$\\times$ \\\\\n201\t\\bottomrule\n202\t\\end{tabular}\n203\t\\end{table}\n204\t\n205\t\\subsection{Cross-Model Validation}\n206\t\n207\tWe replicate the core analysis with LLaMA-3.3-70B-Instruct on Few-NERD (Table~\\ref{tab:crossmodel}). The pairwise correlation is positive and significant ($r=0.09$, $p=0.012$), and the top confused pair is identical (\\emph{person-other} $\\leftrightarrow$ \\emph{person-politician}). The weaker effect size ($r=0.09$ vs.\\ $0.156$) reflects that different model families have different sensitivities to embedding-based SCS as a confusion proxy. The directional consistency across models confirms the phenomenon is not model-specific.\n208\t\n209\t\\begin{table}[t]\n210\t\\centering\n211\t\\small\n212\t\\caption{Cross-model comparison of pairwise BCS--confusion analysis on Few-NERD. Direction is consistent; effect size varies.}\n213\t\\label{tab:crossmodel}\n214\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n215\t\\toprule\n216\tMetric & Qwen3-32B & LLaMA-70B & Consistent? \\\\\n217\t\\midrule\n218\tPairwise $r$ (all) & 0.156 & 0.09 & Yes \\\\\n219\tPairwise $p$ (all) & $4.6\\text{e-}12$ & 0.012 & Both sig. \\\\\n220\tNonzero $\\rho$ & 0.254 & 0.17 & Yes \\\\\n221\tConfusion ratio (c12) & 9.9$\\times$ & 7.5$\\times$ & Yes \\\\\n222\tTop pair & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{person-other $\\leftrightarrow$ politician} & Same \\\\\n223\t\\bottomrule\n224\t\\end{tabular}\n225\t\\end{table}\n226\t\n227\t\\paragraph{Per-type analysis.}\n228\tPer-type partial correlation (controlling for type frequency) shows $r=0.14$ ($p=0.11$) on MAVEN and $r=0.15$ ($p=0.082$) on LLaMA-3.3-70B---directionally correct but only marginal. This is consistent across datasets: per-type aggregation weakens the signal relative to pairwise analysis, as each type's F1 reflects confusion with \\emph{all} partners, diluting the per-pair mechanistic signal.\n229\t\n230\t\\paragraph{Methodological note on frequency confound.}\n231\tBatch-level analysis (F1 vs.\\ BCS within fixed count) yields a \\emph{positive} correlation ($r=0.19$, $p=0.24$ on MAVEN)---opposite to the naive prediction. This is a frequency confound: high-frequency types tend to have both high BCS (more semantic neighbors) and high F1 (more training signal in pretraining). The progression from batch-level to pairwise analysis---which is immune to this confound---demonstrates the importance of appropriate analysis granularity.\n232\t\n233\t%==============================================================================\n234\t\\section{Claim 2: Multi-Channel Degradation Model}\n235\t\\label{sec:claim2}\n236\t%==============================================================================\n237\t\n238\t\\subsection{2$\\times$2 Factorial Design}\n239\t\n240\tWe test the additivity of count and confusability using a $\\{\\text{count}=6, \\text{count}=22\\} \\times \\{\\text{low BCS}, \\text{high BCS}\\}$ factorial design on Few-NERD (Table~\\ref{tab:factorial}).\n241\t\n242\t\\begin{table}[t]\n243\t\\centering\n244\t\\small\n245\t\\caption{$2\\times2$ factorial cell means (F1). Both main effects are significant; their interaction is not.}\n246\t\\label{tab:factorial}\n247\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n248\t\\toprule\n249\t & Low BCS & High BCS & BCS Effect \\\\\n250\t\\midrule\n251\tCount$=6$ & .338 & .280 & $-$.058 \\\\\n252\tCount$=22$ & .318 & .245 & $-$.073 \\\\\n253\t\\midrule\n254\tCount Effect & $-$.020 & $-$.035 & \\\\\n255\t\\bottomrule\n256\t\\end{tabular}\n257\t\\end{table}\n258\t\n259\tTwo-way ANOVA (Table~\\ref{tab:anova}) reveals significant main effects for both count ($F=8.2$, $p=0.006$, $\\eta^2=0.12$) and BCS ($F=5.1$, $p=0.028$, $\\eta^2=0.08$), but no significant interaction ($F=2.3$, $p=0.135$, $\\eta^2=0.04$). The additive prediction for the high-count/high-BCS cell (0.250) closely matches the observed value (0.245), with a residual of only $-0.005$.\n260\t\n261\t\\begin{table}[t]\n262\t\\centering\n263\t\\small\n264\t\\caption{Two-way ANOVA results. Count and BCS are independently significant; their interaction is not, supporting the additive multi-channel model.}\n265\t\\label{tab:anova}\n266\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n267\t\\toprule\n268\tSource & $F$ & $p$ & $\\eta^2$ & Verdict \\\\\n269\t\\midrule\n270\tCount (main) & 8.2 & .006 & .12 & Significant \\\\\n271\tBCS (main) & 5.1 & .028 & .08 & Significant \\\\\n272\tCount $\\times$ BCS & 2.3 & .135 & .04 & Not sig. \\\\\n273\t\\bottomrule\n274\t\\end{tabular}\n275\t\\end{table}\n276\t\n277\tChannel decomposition is clear: count primarily affects recall ($\\Delta=-0.160$), while BCS primarily affects precision ($\\Delta=-0.081$). However, confusability also has a secondary recall effect ($\\Delta=-0.043$), suggesting that when the model is uncertain between confusable types, it sometimes chooses to output nothing.\n278\t\n279\t\\subsection{Error Taxonomy by BCS Bin}\n280\t\n281\tWe classify all false positives and false negatives into four error categories and stratify by BCS bin (Table~\\ref{tab:error_taxonomy}).\n282\t\n283\t\\begin{table}[t]\n284\t\\centering\n285\t\\small\n286\t\\caption{Error distribution by BCS bin on Few-NERD. Type substitution increases monotonically with BCS ($\\chi^2$ $p=0.001$); false negatives decrease.}\n287\t\\label{tab:error_taxonomy}\n288\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n289\t\\toprule\n290\tBCS Bin & TypeSub & Boundary & FalseNeg & Halluc. \\\\\n291\t\\midrule\n292\t$[0, 0.3)$ & 18\\% & 15\\% & 55\\% & 12\\% \\\\\n293\t$[0.3, 0.5)$ & 30\\% & 14\\% & 45\\% & 11\\% \\\\\n294\t$[0.5, 0.7)$ & 42\\% & 11\\% & 38\\% & 9\\% \\\\\n295\t$[0.7, 1.0]$ & 52\\% & 13\\% & 28\\% & 7\\% \\\\\n296\t\\bottomrule\n297\t\\end{tabular}\n298\t\\end{table}\n299\t\n300\tType substitution errors increase monotonically from 18\\% (low BCS) to 52\\% (high BCS), while false negatives decrease from 55\\% to 28\\%. This directly confirms the multi-channel model: confusability drives type confusion (Channel~2) while count/attention dilution drives entity omission (Channel~1). The association between type substitution rate and BCS bin is statistically significant ($\\chi^2=18.4$, $p=0.001$).\n301\t\n302\tBoundary errors remain approximately flat across BCS bins (11--15\\%), confirming they are driven by surface-form ambiguity rather than type confusability. Hallucination rates show a slight decrease (12\\% to 7\\%) that is not monotonic and not significant ($p=0.18$). We characterize these as the \\emph{dominant error modes shifting} with confusability, rather than exclusive channels.\n303\t\n304\t%==============================================================================\n305\t\\section{Claim 3: SDGD Effectiveness}\n306\t\\label{sec:claim3}\n307\t%==============================================================================\n308\t\n309\t\\subsection{SDGD Results}\n310\t\n311\tTable~\\ref{tab:sdgd_main} reports the full comparison across methods and datasets.\n312\t\n313\t\\begin{table*}[t]\n314\t\\centering\n315\t\\small\n316\t\\caption{Full comparison across methods. SDGD-cal achieves 95--99\\% of per-type quality at $\\sim$20\\% of the API cost. $p$ values are vs.\\ the best method (SDGD-cal).}\n317\t\\label{tab:sdgd_main}\n318\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccccr}\n319\t\\toprule\n320\tDataset & Method & $k$ & F1 & Prec. & Recall & WrongType & API Calls \\\\\n321\t\\midrule\n322\t\\multirow{5}{*}{Few-NERD (66)} & Single-pass & 1 & .414 & .648 & .304 & 2,050 & 500 \\\\\n323\t & Random ($k$=10) & 10 & .423 & .395 & .455 & 1,650 & 5,000 \\\\\n324\t & SDGD-zero & 10 & .437 & .405 & .474 & 1,280 & 5,000 \\\\\n325\t & SDGD-cal & 11 & \\textbf{.449} & .415 & .488 & 1,100 & 5,528 \\\\\n326\t & Per-type & 66 & .470 & .385 & .602 & 650 & 33,000 \\\\\n327\t\\midrule\n328\t\\multirow{5}{*}{MAVEN (168)} & Single-pass & 1 & .135 & .340 & .082 & 11,500 & 710 \\\\\n329\t & Random ($k$=22) & 22 & .155 & .175 & .138 & 6,800 & 15,620 \\\\\n330\t & SDGD-zero & 22 & .175 & .190 & .162 & 4,200 & 15,620 \\\\\n331\t & SDGD-cal & 24 & \\textbf{.192} & .205 & .180 & 3,500 & 16,964 \\\\\n332\t & Per-type & 168 & .195 & .150 & .280 & 1,800 & 119,280 \\\\\n333\t\\bottomrule\n334\t\\end{tabular}\n335\t\\end{table*}\n336\t\n337\tOn MAVEN, SDGD-zero yields $+4.0$ F1 over single-pass ($p=0.002$) and $+2.0$ over random batching ($p=0.038$). SDGD-cal further improves to F1$=0.192$, nearly matching per-type splitting ($0.195$, $p=0.82$) at 14\\% of the API cost (16,964 vs.\\ 119,280 calls).\n338\t\n339\tOn Few-NERD, gains are more modest: SDGD-zero yields $+2.3$ F1 over single-pass ($p=0.003$), consistent with the smaller schema (66 types) providing less room for confusability-aware optimization.\n340\t\n341\t\\subsection{Scaling with Schema Complexity}\n342\t\n343\tThe confusability-aware contribution scales with schema size. Table~\\ref{tab:cross_dataset_gain} summarizes gains across datasets.\n344\t\n345\t\\begin{table}[t]\n346\t\\centering\n347\t\\small\n348\t\\caption{SDGD gains scale with schema size: larger schemas benefit more from confusability-aware decomposition.}\n349\t\\label{tab:cross_dataset_gain}\n350\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n351\t\\toprule\n352\tDataset & Types & $\\Delta$F1 & $\\Delta$WrongType \\\\\n353\t\\midrule\n354\tFew-NERD & 66 & $+$2.3 & $-$37.5\\% \\\\\n355\tMAVEN & 168 & $+$4.0 & $-$63.5\\% \\\\\n356\t\\bottomrule\n357\t\\end{tabular}\n358\t\\end{table}\n359\t\n360\tWrong-type errors decrease by 37.5\\% on Few-NERD and 63.5\\% on MAVEN, confirming that confusability-aware grouping directly targets the type confusion mechanism.\n361\t\n362\t\\subsection{Random Batching Ablation}\n363\t\n364\tTable~\\ref{tab:ablation_random} isolates the two contributions of SDGD: batch size reduction (Channel~1 mitigation, captured by random batching) and confusability-aware grouping (Channel~2 mitigation, the SDGD-specific contribution).\n365\t\n366\t\\begin{table}[t]\n367\t\\centering\n368\t\\small\n369\t\\caption{Random vs.\\ SDGD ablation. On MAVEN, confusability-aware grouping contributes $+2.0$ F1 beyond mere batch count reduction ($p=0.018$).}\n370\t\\label{tab:ablation_random}\n371\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n372\t\\toprule\n373\tDataset & Single & Random & SDGD & $p$ \\\\\n374\t\\midrule\n375\tFew-NERD & .414 & .423$\\pm$.008 & .437 & .08 \\\\\n376\tMAVEN & .135 & .155$\\pm$.006 & .175 & .018 \\\\\n377\t\\bottomrule\n378\t\\end{tabular}\n379\t\\end{table}\n380\t\n381\tOn MAVEN, the SDGD-specific contribution ($+2.0$ over random) is significant ($p=0.018$) and equals the batch-reduction contribution ($+2.0$ from single to random). SDGD reduces mean within-batch BCS by 38--41\\% compared to random batching (0.145 vs.\\ 0.235 on MAVEN).\n382\t\n383\tOn Few-NERD, the SDGD-specific contribution ($+1.4$) is marginal ($p=0.08$), consistent with fewer high-SCS pairs in a 66-type schema.\n384\t\n385\t\\subsection{Per-Type Splitting as Upper Bound}\n386\t\n387\tPer-type splitting (one type per batch) provides a quality upper bound but is cost-prohibitive. On MAVEN, SDGD-cal achieves 98.5\\% of per-type F1 (0.192 vs.\\ 0.195) at 14\\% of the API cost (Table~\\ref{tab:sdgd_main}). Per-type splitting also suffers from severe merge conflicts: 72\\% of documents on Few-NERD and 85\\% on MAVEN have span conflicts across passes, introducing resolution errors.\n388\t\n389\t\\subsection{SDGD-cal: Calibration-Enhanced Decomposition}\n390\t\n391\tSDGD-cal replaces embedding SCS with an empirical confusion matrix estimated from 8 calibration examples. On MAVEN, this yields $+1.7$ F1 over SDGD-zero ($p=0.045$), discovering 128 empirical confusion pairs with only 48\\% overlap with SCS top-100 predictions (Table~\\ref{tab:sdgd_cal}).\n392\t\n393\t\\begin{table}[t]\n394\t\\centering\n395\t\\small\n396\t\\caption{SDGD-cal vs.\\ SDGD-zero. Calibration provides larger gains on MAVEN where SCS is less accurate ($\\rho=0.34$ vs.\\ $0.42$ on Few-NERD).}\n397\t\\label{tab:sdgd_cal}\n398\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccl}\n399\t\\toprule\n400\tMethod & F1 & Prec. & Recall & Cal.\\ Cost \\\\\n401\t\\midrule\n402\t\\multicolumn{5}{l}{\\emph{Few-NERD}} \\\\\n403\tSDGD-zero & .437 & .405 & .474 & 0 \\\\\n404\tSDGD-cal & .449 & .415 & .488 & 528 calls \\\\\n405\t$\\Delta$ & $+$1.2 & $+$1.0 & $+$1.4 & \\\\\n406\t\\midrule\n407\t\\multicolumn{5}{l}{\\emph{MAVEN}} \\\\\n408\tSDGD-zero & .175 & .190 & .162 & 0 \\\\\n409\tSDGD-cal & .192 & .205 & .180 & 1,344 calls \\\\\n410\t$\\Delta$ & $+$1.7 & $+$1.5 & $+$1.8 & \\\\\n411\t\\bottomrule\n412\t\\end{tabular}\n413\t\\end{table}\n414\t\n415\tThe gain is larger on MAVEN because SCS is a weaker proxy for FrameNet event types ($\\rho=0.34$) than for NER types ($\\rho=0.42$): frame-semantic distinctions (e.g., \\emph{Giving} vs.\\ \\emph{Commerce\\_sell}) are poorly captured by name embeddings.\n416\t\n417\t%==============================================================================\n418\t\\section{SCS Validation}\n419\t\\label{sec:scs_validation}\n420\t%==============================================================================\n421\t\n422\tWe validate the SCS proxy by comparing embedding-based similarities against empirical confusion matrices from calibration runs (Table~\\ref{tab:scs_valid}).\n423\t\n424\t\\begin{table}[t]\n425\t\\centering\n426\t\\small\n427\t\\caption{SCS validation: embedding similarity vs.\\ empirical confusion. SCS is a moderate proxy ($\\rho=0.38$), stronger on Few-NERD than MAVEN.}\n428\t\\label{tab:scs_valid}\n429\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n430\t\\toprule\n431\tMetric & Few-NERD & MAVEN & Overall \\\\\n432\t\\midrule\n433\tSpearman $\\rho$ & 0.42 & 0.34 & 0.38 \\\\\n434\tKendall $\\tau$ & 0.30 & 0.24 & 0.27 \\\\\n435\tTop-50 Precision & 0.68 & 0.56 & 0.62 \\\\\n436\tTop-50 Recall & 0.50 & 0.40 & 0.45 \\\\\n437\t\\bottomrule\n438\t\\end{tabular}\n439\t\\end{table}\n440\t\n441\tSCS overestimates confusability for type pairs with distinctive lexical cues (e.g., \\emph{org-religion} $\\leftrightarrow$ \\emph{org-political}, SCS$=0.72$ but 0 empirical confusion---entity mentions like ``Church of X'' vs.\\ ``X Party'' are easily distinguished). SCS underestimates confusion for pairs sharing entity surface forms (e.g., \\emph{art-film} $\\leftrightarrow$ \\emph{art-broadcastprogram}, SCS$=0.38$ but 12 confusions---titles like ``Breaking Bad'' can be either).\n442\t\n443\tThese failure modes explain why SDGD-cal improves over SDGD-zero: calibration corrects both false positives (35 overestimates) and false negatives (28 underestimates) in the SCS ranking.\n444\t\n445\t%==============================================================================\n446\t\\section{DocRED: A Boundary Condition}\n447\t\\label{sec:docred}\n448\t%==============================================================================\n449\t\n450\tWe test on DocRED (96 relation types) as a scaling stress test. The pairwise correlation is marginal ($r=0.055$, $p=0.065$). Recall degrades monotonically with count (0.21 at count$=1$ to 0.028 at count$=96$), confirming Channel~1 is task-agnostic. However, the confusability signal is obscured by task-specific confounds: document-level RE requires multi-hop reasoning and entity-pair identification, introducing noise that overwhelms the type confusion signal at the very low absolute F1 levels (0.04--0.10) observed in training-free RE. We interpret this as a boundary condition: confusability analysis requires sufficient base extraction quality for the signal to emerge.\n451\t\n452\t%==============================================================================\n453\t\\section{Discussion}\n454\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n455\t%==============================================================================\n456\t\n457\t\\paragraph{Effect size variation.}\n458\tThe pairwise BCS--confusion correlation varies from $r=0.156$ (Few-NERD, Qwen3-32B) to $r=0.09$ (MAVEN; LLaMA-3.3-70B). This reflects two factors: (1) larger schemas dilute per-pair confusion rates, reducing correlation magnitude, and (2) different models' internal representations align differently with static embedding SCS. The consistent \\emph{direction} across all conditions---positive correlation, same top confused pairs---is more informative than the varying magnitude.\n459\t\n460\t\\paragraph{Additivity with marginal interaction.}\n461\tThe non-significant interaction ($p=0.135$) supports the additive model, but the slight super-additive residual ($-0.005$) and $\\eta^2=0.04$ suggest that at higher counts, confusability's impact may be amplified. Our cell size ($n=12$) provides limited power to detect small interactions; larger-scale factorial designs could resolve this.\n462\t\n463\t\\paragraph{Applicability scope.}\n464\tSDGD's gains scale with schema complexity: significant on 168 types (MAVEN), marginal on 66 types (Few-NERD), and likely unnecessary below 30 types where single-pass performs adequately. The framework is most valuable when the schema exceeds the model's effective discrimination capacity.\n465\t\n466\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n467\t(1) Absolute F1 remains low---SDGD narrows the gap to supervised methods but does not close it; remaining errors stem from non-confusability sources.\n468\t(2) The API cost of multi-pass extraction (10--22$\\times$ single-pass) may be prohibitive for some applications, though SDGD-zero requires no calibration data.\n469\t(3) SCS as a confusability proxy has moderate accuracy ($\\rho=0.38$); better confusability measures (e.g., LLM-internal representations) could improve SDGD.\n470\t\n471\t%==============================================================================\n472\t\\section{Conclusion}\n473\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n474\t%==============================================================================\n475\t\n476\tWe present the first controlled study of multi-channel schema degradation in training-free LLM IE. Our findings show that type confusability contributes to extraction degradation beyond count, with strongest evidence at the type-pair mechanistic level. Count and confusability operate through largely additive channels---count drives recall decline, confusability drives type confusion---enabling independent mitigation. SDGD operationalizes confusability-aware decomposition via graph coloring, achieving significant gains on complex schemas ($+4.0$ F1 on MAVEN's 168 types) while matching per-type splitting quality at $\\sim$20\\% of the cost. The scaling pattern---larger schemas benefit more---suggests that SDGD's practical value will increase as IE applications target increasingly complex ontologies.\n477\t\n478\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n479\t\n480\t\n481\t\\end{document}\n482\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01VtHaaJrH1gouPjpSidGSLX","session_id":"99db7f36-0c41-4b00-81a4-b83fd43f6915","uuid":"58f6f5ca-b824-40f7-9c3b-1d2ca796ee82","timestamp":"2026-05-15T06:28:32.244Z"}
