{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XEBSNfyd77oB9rB1zSirdc","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260515T053106\n3\ttier: best\n4\toverall_score: 4 - Weak Accept\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-15T06:18:29.408390+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Dream Review Report: Multi-Channel Schema Degradation in Training-Free LLM IE\n9\t\n10\t## Paper\n11\t\"Multi-Channel Schema Degradation in Training-Free LLM Information Extraction: Isolating Confusability from Count and Operationalizing SDGD\"\n12\t\n13\t## Review Process\n14\tFull roundtable: 2 reviewers (R1: Methods+Experiments, R2: Novelty+Related Work) → independent reviews → cross-sharing → convergence → synthesis.\n15\t\n16\t---\n17\t\n18\t## Consolidated Summary\n19\t\n20\tThe paper studies why training-free LLM information extraction degrades on complex schemas. It proposes a multi-channel degradation model: Channel 1 (type count → recall decline via capacity bottleneck) and Channel 2 (type confusability → type substitution via semantic interference). Across two tasks (NER on Few-NERD/66 types, ED on MAVEN/168 types), two model families (Qwen3-32B, LLaMA-3.3-70B), and a supplementary third task (RE on DocRED/96 types), the paper establishes that these channels are orthogonal and additive (2×2 factorial interaction p=0.82). BCS independently predicts F1 degradation after controlling for type frequency (partial r=0.32 on Few-NERD, r=0.22 on MAVEN, both p<0.005). The paper operationalizes these findings into SDGD (Schema-DAG Guided Decomposition), a graph-coloring framework that partitions types into low-confusability batches, achieving +7.6 F1 on Few-NERD and +6.0 on MAVEN over single-pass baselines.\n21\t\n22\t---\n23\t\n24\t## Experiment Coverage\n25\t\n26\tAll 12 planned experiments are reflected in the paper:\n27\t- exp-004 (MAVEN replication): Tables 1-3, Sections 5.1-5.2 ✓\n28\t- exp-005 (DocRED generalization): Section 5.5 ✓\n29\t- exp-006 (2×2 factorial): Table 5, Section 5.3 ✓\n30\t- exp-007 (Error taxonomy): Table 6, Section 5.3 ✓\n31\t- exp-012 (LLaMA validation): Table 4, Section 5.2 ✓\n32\t- exp-008 (SDGD-zero Few-NERD): Table 7, Section 5.4 ✓\n33\t- exp-009 (SDGD-zero MAVEN): Table 8, Section 5.4 ✓\n34\t- exp-010 (SDGD-cal Few-NERD): Table 7, Section 5.4 ✓\n35\t- exp-011 (SDGD-cal MAVEN): Table 8, Section 5.4 ✓\n36\t- exp-013 (Random batching ablation): Tables 7-9, Section 6 ✓\n37\t- exp-014 (Per-type splitting): Tables 7-8, Section 6 ✓\n38\t- exp-015 (SCS proxy validation): Section 5.6 ✓\n39\t\n40\tCoverage is **complete**. All planned experiments appear with expected-tier numbers.\n41\t\n42\t---\n43\t\n44\t## Strengths (Consensus)\n45\t\n46\t**S1. Well-designed controlled experiments (both reviewers).** The 2×2 factorial design (Table 5) with ANOVA interaction testing is the right methodology for the additivity claim. The near-zero interaction (F=0.05, p=0.82) is convincing. The error taxonomy crossover (Table 6: TypeSub 8%→68%, FalseNeg 62%→15%, χ²=142.3, p=2.1e-24) provides the paper's strongest mechanistic evidence — it moves beyond correlation to identify distinct error signatures for each channel. These two experiments are the paper's backbone.\n47\t\n48\t**S2. Transparent confound handling (both reviewers).** The discovery that batch-level BCS-F1 correlation is *positive* (opposite to hypothesis) due to type frequency confounding, and the subsequent pivot to pairwise and per-type analyses, is commendably reported. This kind of methodological honesty is rare and significantly strengthens credibility. The three-layer evidence hierarchy (pairwise → per-type partial → error taxonomy) is well-structured.\n49\t\n50\t**S3. Cross-task and cross-model replication (both reviewers).** Testing on NER (Few-NERD), ED (MAVEN), and RE (DocRED) across two model families demonstrates the phenomenon is not task-specific or model-specific. The 7/10 overlap in top confused pairs between Qwen3-32B and LLaMA-3.3-70B, with confusion matrix rank correlation ρ=0.61, supports the claim that confusability is schema-intrinsic.\n51\t\n52\t**S4. Comprehensive ablation structure (R1).** The gain decomposition (Table 9) cleanly separates count-reduction (+4.1 F1) and confusability-reduction (+3.5 F1) contributions. The near-perfect additivity (4.1+3.5=7.6) is consistent with the factorial findings. Random batching ablation is the correct control.\n53\t\n54\t**S5. Practical zero-shot deployability (both reviewers).** SDGD-zero requires no labeled data and is robust to threshold selection (τ ∈ [0.3, 0.6] yields <1pp F1 variation). The cost-quality tradeoff — matching per-type splitting at ~22% API cost — is practically meaningful.\n55\t\n56\t**S6. Genuinely novel decomposition of schema complexity (R2).** The explicit isolation of count from confusability as independent degradation channels with distinct error signatures (omission vs. type substitution) is a real conceptual contribution beyond treating schema complexity as monolithic.\n57\t\n58\t---\n59\t\n60\t## Weaknesses (Consensus + Validated Individual)\n61\t\n62\t### Major\n63\t\n64\t**W1. Small pairwise effect sizes (both reviewers, HIGH PRIORITY).** Pairwise r=0.156 (Few-NERD) and r=0.19 (MAVEN) explain only 2.4% and 3.6% of variance respectively. The paper leans heavily on statistical significance inflated by large sample sizes (n=1,953 and n=14,028 pairs). While the per-type partial correlations are stronger (r=0.32, r=0.22), and the error taxonomy provides mechanistic rather than just correlational evidence, the paper needs to be more forthright about the modest predictive power of BCS as a standalone metric. The gap between \"statistically significant predictor\" and \"practically useful predictor\" should be discussed.\n65\t\n66\t**W2. Missing LLM-PEE head-to-head comparison (both reviewers, HIGH PRIORITY).** LLM-PEE already performs embedding-similarity-based type partitioning for LLM IE. The algorithmic difference between \"partition by similarity\" (LLM-PEE) and \"partition by confusability via graph coloring\" (SDGD-zero) may be small in practice — both produce batches of semantically dissimilar types. Without a direct comparison on the same datasets, it is impossible to assess SDGD's marginal contribution over existing methods. This is the most important missing baseline.\n67\t\n68\t**W3. Low absolute performance undermines practical impact claims (both reviewers).** MAVEN F1=0.14-0.16 (even with SDGD-cal) is far below any practical utility threshold. Few-NERD F1=0.49-0.51 is 30+ points below supervised baselines. The paper should position itself primarily as a *diagnostic and analytical* contribution, with SDGD as proof-of-concept operationalization, rather than framing SDGD as a production-ready deployment tool.\n69\t\n70\t**W4. Novelty framing slightly overstated (R2, validated).** That increasing class count hurts recall and that semantically similar classes cause confusion are individually well-known in classification literature. The contribution is demonstrating and quantifying these in *training-free LLM IE with in-prompt schema presentation* — but the \"multi-channel degradation model\" framing implies more theoretical novelty than exists. The channels are post-hoc labels for two known failure modes, not derived from a formal model of LLM computation. The paper should distinguish \"first controlled demonstration in this setting\" from \"discovery of a new phenomenon.\"\n71\t\n72\t**W5. No variance reporting for SDGD results (R1, validated).** Tables 7-8 report single-point F1 estimates without confidence intervals or standard deviations. The random batching baseline reportedly uses 5 seeds (std=0.012), but SDGD results appear to be single-run. Without variance estimates, the statistical significance of SDGD-zero vs. random (+3.5pp) is unclear beyond the reported paired t-test.\n73\t\n74\t**W6. Only 2 LLMs tested (R2, validated).** Qwen3-32B and LLaMA-3.3-70B share similar architectural foundations (dense transformer). No proprietary APIs (GPT-4, Claude) and no smaller models (<7B). The generalizability claim would be substantially stronger with a third model from a different paradigm.\n75\t\n76\t### Minor\n77\t\n78\t**W7. \"DAG\" misnomer (both reviewers).** The confusability graph is undirected and the algorithm is graph coloring on an undirected graph. \"Schema-DAG\" in the title is misleading. Should be corrected.\n79\t\n80\t**W8. Broken citations (R2).** All references appear as [?]. This would be a desk-reject issue at any venue. While likely a compilation artifact, it prevents verification of citation completeness.\n81\t\n82\t**W9. \"First controlled study\" claim needs qualification (R2).** IEPile constructs hard-negative schemas during training; the Familiarity metric studies label similarity effects. The claim should be narrowed to \"first controlled study in training-free, inference-time LLM IE.\"\n83\t\n84\t**W10. SDGD-cal is arguably few-shot (R2).** Using 8-10 calibration examples moves beyond \"training-free.\" The paper should clarify the terminology.\n85\t\n86\t**W11. Incomplete confound controls (R1).** The paper controls for type frequency but not: (a) type definition/label length, (b) hierarchical depth (fine-grained subtypes are both more confusable AND harder), (c) entity/event base rate. Few-NERD's taxonomy structure potentially confounds BCS with hierarchical proximity.\n87\t\n88\t**W12. WrongType counts not normalized (R1).** Absolute WrongType counts in count curves naturally increase with type count (more types = more confusion opportunities). Should normalize by number of type pairs or predictions.\n89\t\n90\t**W13. Graph coloring is algorithmically standard (both reviewers).** Greedy graph coloring on a thresholded similarity graph is not a methodological contribution. The contribution is the empirical finding and the principled application, which should be framed accordingly.\n91\t\n92\t---\n93\t\n94\t## Questions for Authors\n95\t\n96\t1. **LLM-PEE comparison**: Can you provide a direct head-to-head comparison between SDGD-zero and LLM-PEE's similarity-based partitioning on Few-NERD and MAVEN? What is the concrete difference in partition quality?\n97\t\n98\t2. **Sentence encoder**: What sentence encoder produces the SCS embeddings? This is critical for reproducibility and for understanding the SCS proxy quality.\n99\t\n100\t3. **Variance**: Can you report confidence intervals or standard deviations for all methods in Tables 7-8 across multiple document samples or random seeds?\n101\t\n102\t4. **Confound controls**: Have you checked whether controlling for type label length or hierarchical depth (in addition to frequency) changes the per-type partial correlation results?\n103\t\n104\t5. **Merge/dedup**: How exactly does cross-batch deduplication work? What confidence scores are used? Table 7 notes dedup impact is ~0.8pp — is this consistent across datasets?\n105\t\n106\t6. **Prompt format interaction**: Do findings hold across different prompt formats (code-style à la GoLLIE vs. natural language)? Schema presentation format could modulate both channels.\n107\t\n108\t7. **Factorial cell sizes**: What are the sample sizes per cell in the 2×2 factorial? Small cells limit ANOVA power.\n109\t\n110\t8. **Batch-level positive correlation implications**: Does the positive batch-level BCS-F1 correlation (before controlling for frequency) mean SDGD could sometimes separate high-frequency, naturally co-occurring types in a way that hurts their extraction?\n111\t\n112\t---\n113\t\n114\t## Reviewer Disagreements & Resolution\n115\t\n116\t**R1 flagged \"numerical inconsistencies across versions\"** as the most critical weakness, citing discrepancies like per-type partial r=0.15 (p=0.082) vs. r=0.32 (p=0.002). **Director assessment: This criticism is invalid.** The paper presents a single consistent set of numbers that matches the experiment plan. R1 appears to have hallucinated alternative numbers, possibly confusing the paper's actual values with hypothetical weaker results. All numbers in the paper are internally consistent. This weakness is **discarded** from the final assessment.\n117\t\n118\t**R2 claimed random batching comparison is missing.** **Director assessment: This criticism is invalid.** Random batching results are clearly reported in Tables 7 (F1=0.455), Table 8 (F1=0.11), and Table 9 (gain decomposition). R2 missed these. This weakness is **discarded**.\n119\t\n120\t**R1 claimed BCS is defined inconsistently (max vs. mean).** **Director assessment: This criticism is invalid.** The paper defines BCS as mean pairwise SCS in Eq. 2 and uses this consistently. This weakness is **discarded**.\n121\t\n122\t---\n123\t\n124\t## Overall Assessment\n125\t\n126\t### Score: 4 — Weak Accept\n127\t\n128\t### Justification\n129\tThe paper asks an important and underexplored question — what specifically makes complex schemas hard for training-free LLM IE — and provides a methodologically careful answer through well-designed controlled experiments. The 2×2 factorial (interaction p=0.82) and error taxonomy crossover (χ²=142.3) are the standout results, providing convincing evidence for orthogonal degradation channels. The three-layer evidence structure and transparent confound handling demonstrate above-average scientific rigor. SDGD is a clean, practical operationalization with a compelling cost-quality tradeoff.\n130\t\n131\tHowever, the paper falls short of a clear accept for several reasons: (1) pairwise effect sizes are small (r≈0.16, ~2.5% variance), raising questions about the practical predictive power of BCS; (2) the most important competing baseline (LLM-PEE, which already performs similarity-based type partitioning) is not compared; (3) absolute performance on MAVEN (F1=0.14-0.16) is too low for practical deployment, making the practical impact narrative weaker; (4) the novelty is more demonstrational than conceptual — the individual phenomena are known, and graph coloring is standard; (5) only 2 models are tested. The paper is a solid empirical contribution that would benefit from the LLM-PEE comparison, more candid effect-size discussion, and a framing that emphasizes the diagnostic/analytical contribution over the practical tool narrative.\n132\t\n133\t### Recommendation\n134\tSuitable for EMNLP 2026 main with revisions addressing W1 (effect size discussion), W2 (LLM-PEE comparison), and W3 (reframing practical claims). Current form is borderline — closer to Findings than main conference without the LLM-PEE comparison.\n135\t\n136\t### Confidence: 4/5\n137\tBoth reviewers are confident in their assessments of experimental methodology and positioning. Main uncertainty is whether LLM-PEE comparison would change the relative contribution assessment."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01VtHaaJrH1gouPjpSidGSLX","session_id":"99db7f36-0c41-4b00-81a4-b83fd43f6915","uuid":"95e50b11-b80e-4fb9-8195-690dd23befed","timestamp":"2026-05-15T06:28:33.011Z"}
